d slightly backward when opened, exposing the lining surface so it faces up-and-forward toward the viewer, which is the intended look. I then move on to checking the sparkle card frames, bone references, and the dais lathe geometry, confirming the runes plane sits correctly on top of the dais surface with the right orientation.\n\nI verify the large glow plane's radius fits within the dais bounds so the mounds properly hide its inner edges, then check that the gem geometry and crown mound heights align as expected, and confirm the flat colour count stays within the 64-colour budget.\n\nThen I estimate total triangle counts: roughly 400 coins at ~42 tris each (~17k), mound spheres contributing ~1.5k, and gems adding another ~2k, tallying things up to make sure the scene stays within a reasonable polygon budget.\n\nTallying up part counts, I land around 200-220 parts after accounting for merges—dais, rim pillars, mounds, chest hardware, and so on. The brief wants rich detail, so I'm weighing whether to add a few more embellishments like engraved decals or wood nails on the lid, while being careful not to overdo it since I can't actually render and check the result.\n\nI'm also doing a quick mental pass over the code for runtime errors—checking that geometry transforms, the aim/quaternion logic for spin, normal tilting, scatter filters, and surface calls on the gold chest part are all correctly structured and won't throw.\n\n(n) Checking name duplication - each part builder takes a name, and explicit duplicate names across parts might still be allowed by the GLB format even if unusual, so that's probably not a blocker.\n\n",
